body","enumerated":true,"label":"list_item","marker":"1)","orig":"1) rate of_wood residue application: 0, 45,","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":672.3333333333334,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":52.333333333333336,"r":255.0,"t":685.0},"charspan":[0,43],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"rate of_wood residue application: 0, 45,"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","enumerated":true,"label":"list_item","marker":"2)","orig":"2) rate of nitrogen fertilization: 0, 2.5, 5.0 and 7.5 kg of NIMg of wood residue in residue-amended treatments; the nonresidue amended treatment received 0~ 112, 224 and 336 kg Nlha, which was equivalent to the rates of N applied on the 45 Mglha wood rate treatment.","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":564.3333333333334,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.666666666666664,"r":257.0,"t":650.0},"charspan":[0,267],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"rate of nitrogen fertilization: 0, 2.5, 5.0 and 7.5 kg of NIMg of wood residue in residue-amended treatments; the nonresidue amended treatment received 0~ 112, 224 and 336 kg Nlha, which was equivalent to the rates of N applied on the 45 Mglha wood rate treatment."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Initial results indicated that while vegetation was significantly stimulated by the lowest rates of N fertilization, little   subsequent difference in plant response existed among the 3 rates of N application (Smith et al 1985) . For the purposes of the present analysis, therefore, plant responses to non-N fertilization will be compared to the average of responses among the 3 N fertilization rate treatments (i.e., a non-N N-fertilized treatment comparison) .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":432.6666666666667,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":32.0,"r":273.0,"t":554.0},"charspan":[0,462],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Initial results indicated that while vegetation was significantly stimulated by the lowest rates of N fertilization, little   subsequent difference in plant response existed among the 3 rates of N application (Smith et al 1985) . For the purposes of the present analysis, therefore, plant responses to non-N fertilization will be compared to the average of responses among the 3 N fertilization rate treatments (i.e., a non-N N-fertilized treatment comparison) ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Two mixtures   of seeded species {native and introduced perennial  grasses) were sown on the site in 1981 to comprise a seed mix treatment (Smith et al 1985) . This report will present only responses of the native mixture, since this mix was later   selected to receive the gypsum treatments because  of better overall   response to the initial spoil amendments. The native mixture included 5 cool-season perennial grasses ('Rosana' western wheatgrass, Aqropyron smithii; 'Critana' thickspike wheatgrass, A. dasystachyum; Revenue'  slender wheatgrass, A: trachycaulum; Sodar'  streambank wheatgrass, 4 riparium; and 'Lodorm green needlegrass, Stipa viridula) and one shrub (Nuttall saltbush, Atriplex uttallii) . This mixture was seeded at rates to yield 130 live seedslm? for each grass   species and 32 live   seedslm? for Nuttall saltbush.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":361.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":33.0,"r":272.6666666666667,"t":422.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,241],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":565.3333333333334,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":305.0,"r":545.6666666666666,"t":720.3333333333334},"charspan":[242,842],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"Two mixtures   of seeded species {native and introduced perennial  grasses) were sown on the site in 1981 to comprise a seed mix treatment (Smith et al 1985) . This report will present only responses of the native mixture, since this mix was later   selected to receive the gypsum treatments because  of better overall   response to the initial spoil amendments. The native mixture included 5 cool-season perennial grasses ('Rosana' western wheatgrass, Aqropyron smithii; 'Critana' thickspike wheatgrass, A. dasystachyum; Revenue'  slender wheatgrass, A: trachycaulum; Sodar'  streambank wheatgrass, 4 riparium; and 'Lodorm green needlegrass, Stipa viridula) and one shrub (Nuttall saltbush, Atriplex uttallii) . This mixture was seeded at rates to yield 130 live seedslm? for each grass   species and 32 live   seedslm? for Nuttall saltbush."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table 1. Pre-treatment characteristics of bentonite 'Upton, Wyoming study site, 1981 (from Smith 0t al. 1985) . spoils","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":320.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":33.333333333333336,"r":252.84035345989673,"t":350.0},"charspan":[0,118],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Table 1. Pre-treatment characteristics of bentonite 'Upton, Wyoming study site, 1981 (from Smith 0t al. 1985) . spoils"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Site preparation after grading consisted of chisel plowing; application of varied wood residue and N-fertilization rate treatments (plus a uniform plowing to uniformly incorporate all  residue and fertilizer  amendments within the surface 30 cm The seed mixture was subsequently sown with a grain drill in October 1981","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":456.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":305.6666666666667,"r":544.6666666666666,"t":553.0},"charspan":[0,318],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Site preparation after grading consisted of chisel plowing; application of varied wood residue and N-fertilization rate treatments (plus a uniform plowing to uniformly incorporate all  residue and fertilizer  amendments within the surface 30 cm The seed mixture was subsequently sown with a grain drill in October 1981"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In April of 1987, the study was expanded by adding gypsum as a third soil amendment treatment (see Schuman et al 1989 for full detail) . Two gypsum regimes were superimposed by dividing each native species plot, and randomly designating each   portion to receive gYpsum at either 0 or 56 Mglha. The 56 Mglha gypsum rate supplied sufficient Ca ions to reduce the exchangeable sodium percentage (ESP) of the spoil to 15. The gypsum was surface-applied with no subsequent soil incorporation, to avoid physically disrupting previously established vegetation.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":299.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":305.6666666666667,"r":546.3333333333334,"t":445.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,554],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"In April of 1987, the study was expanded by adding gypsum as a third soil amendment treatment (see Schuman et al 1989 for full detail) . Two gypsum regimes were superimposed by dividing each native species plot, and randomly designating each   portion to receive gYpsum at either 0 or 56 Mglha. The 56 Mglha gypsum rate supplied sufficient Ca ions to reduce the exchangeable sodium percentage (ESP) of the spoil to 15. The gypsum was surface-applied with no subsequent soil incorporation, to avoid physically disrupting previously established vegetation."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Samplina (1988 19902","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":277.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":307.0,"r":423.6666666666667,"t":289.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,20],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"Samplina (1988 19902"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Techniques of vegetation sampling and analysis for the pre-gypsum amendment Years of 1981 through 1986 were described in preceeding reports (Smith et al 1985, 1986; Schuman et al 1989; Belden et al 1990). Methods and results of postgypsum amendment soil sampling were described by Meining (1991), and will not be included in this paper.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":168.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":306.6666666666667,"r":546.3333333333334,"t":266.0},"charspan":[0,336],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Techniques of vegetation sampling and analysis for the pre-gypsum amendment Years of 1981 through 1986 were described in preceeding reports (Smith et al 1985, 1986; Schuman et al 1989; Belden et al 1990). Methods and results of postgypsum amendment soil sampling were described by Meining (1991), and will not be included in this paper."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Vegetation was sampled from 1988 through 1990 for density, canopy cover, and aboveground biomass. Within each replicate block, 2 permanent 0.25 m? rectangular quadrat locations were established within each wood residue-fertilizationgypsum treatment combination subplot for density and canopy cover estimation. Stem density was","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":70.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":307.0,"r":547.3333333333334,"t":158.66666666666663},"charspan":[0,326],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"Vegetation was sampled from 1988 through 1990 for density, canopy cover, and aboveground biomass. Within each replicate block, 2 permanent 0.25 m? rectangular quadrat locations were established within each wood residue-fertilizationgypsum treatment combination subplot for density and canopy cover estimation. Stem density was"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"Page   405","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":15.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":272.6666666666667,"r":322.3333333333333,"t":29.66666666666663},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":"Page   405"}],"version":"1.10.0"}